{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Sigmund Freud ( ; ] ; born Sigismund Schlomo Freud; 6 May 1856 – 23 September 1939) was an Austrian neurologist and the founder of psychoanalysis, a clinical method for treating psychopathology through dialogue between a patient and a psychoanalyst.","title":"Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Freud was born to Galician Jewish parents in the Moravian town of Freiberg, in the Austrian Empire.","title":"Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He qualified as a doctor of medicine in 1881 at the University of Vienna.","title":"Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Upon completing his habilitation in 1885, he was appointed a docent in neuropathology and became an affiliated professor in 1902.","title":"Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Freud lived and worked in Vienna, having set up his clinical practice there in 1886.","title":"Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" In 1938 Freud left Austria to escape the Nazis.","title":"Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" He died in exile in the United Kingdom in 1939.","title":"Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Assault on Truth: Freud's Suppression of the Seduction Theory is a book by Jeffrey Moussaieff Masson, in which the author argues that Sigmund Freud deliberately suppressed his early hypothesis, known as the seduction theory, that hysteria is caused by sexual abuse during infancy, because he refused to believe that children are the victims of sexual violence and abuse within their own families.","title":"The Assault on Truth"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Masson reached this conclusion while he had access to some of Freud's unpublished letters as projects director of the Sigmund Freud Archives.","title":"The Assault on Truth"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "The Assault on Truth" was first published in 1984, and several revised editions have since been published.","title":"The Assault on Truth"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Sigmund Freud Prize or Sigmund Freud Prize for Scientific Prose (German "Sigmund Freud-Preis für wissenschaftliche Prosa") is a German literary award named after Sigmund Freud and awarded by the Deutsche Akademie für Sprache und Dichtung.","title":"Sigmund Freud Prize"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was first awarded in 1964.","title":"Sigmund Freud Prize"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Life and Work of Sigmund Freud is a biography of Sigmund Freud by Ernest Jones.","title":"The Life and Work of Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The most famous and influential biography of Freud, "The Life and Work of Sigmund Freud" was originally published in three volumes (first volume 1953, second volume 1955, third volume 1957); a one-volume edition abridged by literary critics Lionel Trilling and Steven Marcus followed in 1961.","title":"The Life and Work of Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" When first published, "The Life and Work of Sigmund Freud" was acclaimed, and sales exceeded expectations.","title":"The Life and Work of Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Although his biography has retained its status as a classic, Jones has been criticized for presenting an overly favorable image of Freud.","title":"The Life and Work of Sigmund Freud"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Sigmund Freud Museum in Vienna is a museum founded in 1971 covering Sigmund Freud's life story.","title":"Sigmund Freud Museum (Vienna)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is located in the Alsergrund district, at Berggasse 19.","title":"Sigmund Freud Museum (Vienna)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 2003 the museum was put in the hands of the newly established Sigmund Freud Foundation, which has since received the entire building as an endowment.","title":"Sigmund Freud Museum (Vienna)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It also covers the history of psychoanalysis.","title":"Sigmund Freud Museum (Vienna)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The statue of Sigmund Freud is a seated bronze statue of the founder of psychoanalysis, Sigmund Freud, situated on a limestone plinth at the junction of Fitzjohn's Avenue and Belsize Lane in Hampstead, North London.","title":"Statue of Sigmund Freud, Hampstead"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Freud lived at nearby 20 Maresfield Gardens, for the last months of his life.","title":"Statue of Sigmund Freud, Hampstead"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" His house is now the Freud Museum.","title":"Statue of Sigmund Freud, Hampstead"}],"peer_messages":["-Preis für wissenschaftliche Prosa\") is a German literary award named after Sigmund Freud and awarded by the Deutsche Akademie für Sprache und Dichtung.","title":"Sigmund Freud Prize"}
